<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- This app draws its entire UI with Jetpack Compose, and every colour it
         uses comes from Material 3's own lightColorScheme() / darkColorScheme()
         through EasyVoiceTheme. The only job left for an XML theme is dressing
         the window before Compose's first frame: the background, so there is no
         flash, and the system bars. -->
    <!-- THIS THEME IS THE LIGHT ONE, AND values-night/styles.xml IS THE DARK
         ONE. The app follows the system now, so the window that is painted
         BEFORE Compose draws has to follow it as well - otherwise a phone in
         light mode opens on a dark rectangle and then swaps, which is a flash
         rather than a handover.

         The parent is the FRAMEWORK theme, not Theme.Material3 from
         com.google.android.material: that View library and androidx.appcompat
         were pulled in for one line and are gone from the build, and the
         activities are ComponentActivity, which needs no AppCompat theme. Only
         android: attributes are set here, because colorPrimary and its family
         are Material Components attributes that do not exist outside that
         library. Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar and Theme.Material.NoActionBar
         both exist in the framework - checked in AOSP's own
         core/res/res/values/themes_material.xml, not recalled.

         windowLightStatusBar is TRUE here and false at night: it asks for DARK
         bar icons, which is what a light window needs.
         windowLightNavigationBar says the same for the other bar and closes a
         gap this theme used to have - it was never stated, so the navigation
         bar's icons were left unspecified. It arrived in API 27 and minSdk is
         24; an older device ignores an attribute it does not know.

         statusBarColor / navigationBarColor are ignored from API 35, where the
         system draws every app edge to edge. They still matter below it, and
         only for the first frame, because EvActivity calls enableEdgeToEdge()
         and androidx makes both bars transparent at runtime. -->
    <style name="AppTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:colorBackground">@color/ev_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/ev_background</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/ev_background</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/ev_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">true</item>
    </style>
    <style name="AppTheme.NoActionBar" parent="@style/AppTheme" />

    <!-- The window the launcher opens. It hands over to AppTheme.NoActionBar as
         soon as the first Compose frame is drawn.

         windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon is @mipmap/ic_launcher, the ADAPTIVE
         icon, on purpose. The platform gives an adaptive splash icon a 240dp
         canvas and shows the inner 160dp after masking - the same two-thirds
         rule the launcher mask uses, which is exactly what
         tools/icon/make_icons.py already inset the badge for: 68% of 240dp is
         163dp, so the badge lands on the visible circle instead of being
         cropped. On API 24-25, which cannot parse mipmap-anydpi-v26, the same
         reference resolves to the full-bleed legacy PNG, which is right there
         too. There is no separate splash asset and there should not be one -
         the splash IS the launcher icon.

         The background is ev_background, the same colour as windowBackground
         and the Compose surface, so the handover is a change of content rather
         than a flash of colour.

         NOTHING holds this screen open. The owner is blind and navigates by
         TalkBack, so the startup scan must not sit behind a picture: there is
         no setKeepOnScreenCondition and no custom exit animation anywhere. The
         splash lasts exactly as long as the first frame takes to draw. -->
    <style name="Theme.EasyVoice.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/ev_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@mipmap/ic_launcher</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme.NoActionBar</item>
    </style>
</resources>
